/* The handoff's stat cell, quoted from the region detail card's three-cell
 * row - "flex: 1; background: #f8f6fc; border-radius: 12px; padding: 9px
 * 10px", with a 9px uppercase micro label over a 12px bold value.
 *
 * WHAT IS TOKENISED AND WHAT IS QUOTED, on ScreenScaffold.uss's convention:
 * the token layer holds the SCALE, and one-off component geometry taken from
 * the handoff is written literally next to the sentence it came from.
 *   - `#f8f6fc` IS --surface-sunk exactly, so it is the token.
 *   - the handoff's per-screen radii range over the 12-16px band it declares
 *     for rows; --radius-row (14px) is the token layer's single answer for
 *     that band, so this is the scale rather than one-off geometry.
 *   - 9px and 10px padding are not on the spacing scale (8 and 12 sit either
 *     side) and 3px is the gap under the label. Rounding them to steps the
 *     handoff did not ask for would change the cell's proportion for the
 *     sake of tidiness, so they are literal, as the scaffold's own 14/10/34
 *     are.
 *
 * THE VALUE'S SIZE IS NOT SET HERE, AND THAT IS A DECISION. `t-num` in
 * Theme.uss sets --text-secondary (11px); the handoff's inline style for
 * this cell says 12px. Overriding it from this file would put the 11px
 * numeral floor - one of bible 10.6's two hard requirements - at the mercy
 * of which single-class rule the cascade happened to apply last, for one
 * pixel. The floor wins; the 1px is recorded here rather than argued with
 * silently.
 */
.stat-cell {
    /* The handoff's `flex: 1`. flex-basis 0 rather than the default auto, so
     * three cells come out equal width instead of sized by their content. */
    flex-grow: 1;
    flex-shrink: 1;
    flex-basis: 0;
    flex-direction: column;
    background-color: var(--surface-sunk);
    border-radius: var(--radius-row);
    padding-top: 9px;
    padding-bottom: 9px;
    padding-left: 10px;
    padding-right: 10px;
    /* HALF THE HANDOFF'S 8px GAP ON EACH SIDE, because a cell cannot own a
     * gap any other way. USS has no `gap` and no `:last-child`, so a single
     * right margin would leak past the last cell in the row - and styling
     * the ROW from this file is not available either: see the note at the
     * foot of this sheet. The cost is 4px of dead space at each end of a
     * row; a caller that wants it flush cancels it with a -4px margin on the
     * container, which is one line at the call site rather than a rule that
     * looks like it works and does not. */
    margin-left: var(--space-1);
    margin-right: var(--space-1);
}

/* No rule for `.stat-cell__label`: `t-micro` already carries all four of the
 * handoff's values for it - 10px, --mute, bold, and the uppercase tracking.
 *
 * The value's own rule is layout plus the handoff's ink; its size and its
 * tabular face come from `t-num`, per the note at the head of this file. */
.stat-cell__value {
    margin-top: 3px;
    color: var(--ink);
}

/* THERE IS NO `.stat-cell-row` RULE IN THIS FILE, AND THERE CANNOT BE ONE.
 *
 * The first version of this component had one - `flex-direction: row` plus
 * a -4px margin on each side to cancel the cells' own - and it compiled, it
 * passed every gate, and it styled nothing at all. A stylesheet attached
 * through `<ui:Style>` in a component's UXML lands on the element the tree
 * is cloned into and applies to THAT ELEMENT AND ITS DESCENDANTS. The
 * container holding a row of cells is the cells' PARENT, so it is outside
 * every sheet the cells carry, and the rule sat inert with nothing to say
 * so. The Components capture is what caught it: the three cells rendered
 * stacked and full width, at zero height, with their values spilling out
 * of the card below them - a flex-basis of 0 in a container that was still
 * a column because the rule that would have made it a row never arrived.
 *
 * WORTH KNOWING BEFORE TASKS 9-12 WRITE ANOTHER ONE. A component's own
 * stylesheet can describe the component and everything inside it, and can
 * describe NOTHING about where it is placed. Layout that belongs to the
 * container - a row of cells, a grid of cards, a gap between siblings - is
 * the container's to own, in the stylesheet of whatever owns the container.
 */
